  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="d7a7-950e-7b51-a441" name="Japanese (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #68 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). At the start of this period, armies were small forces led by warlords and their retainers, the progenitors of the Bushi, fought mounted. During the period armies grew in size, and weaponry began to change; by the later 15th century the Bushi increasingly fought on foot. The Japanese gradually took control of all the Japanese Islands, meddled in Korea and defeated a Mongol invasion; despite this, the most common opponent for a Japanese army would be another Japanese army. The list can be used to create early, middle and late variants. 

ARMY COMPOSITIONS (pick one in the Army composition list):
[Japanese - Early Army 794-1040]
  Cavalry: up to 50%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 50%
  Characters: Up to 25% Cavalry: Up to 50% Infantry: Up to 50% Allies: Up to 25%: Korean List
[Japanese - Japanese]
  Japanese armies do not have an army standard or unit standards. Instead, formed units led by the Army General or a Lord may add +1 Combat Resolution bonus and may re-roll a failed Break Test.
[Japanese - Late Army 1392 –1500]
  Cavalry: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 100%
  Characters: Up to 25%: May have thrusting spear or halberd (+2) Cavalry: Up to 25% Infantry: Up to 100% Allies: Up to 20%: Warrior Monks
[Japanese - Middle Army 1041-1391]
  Cavalry: up to 50%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 75%
  Characters: Up to 25%: May have thrusting spear (+2) Cavalry: Up to 50% Infantry: Up to 75% Allies: Up to 25%: Warrior Monks</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
